        option: [` Arterial dissection`, ` Arteriovenous fistula`, ` Berry aneurysm`,`Renal artery stenosis`,` Fibrinoid necrosis`],
        optionFeedback: [`Arterial dissection classically involves the aorta and occurs at high frequency in patients with Marfan syndrome.`,`Arteriovenous fistula is an abnormal connection between an artery and a vein; it may be congenital or may form during life due to trauma or local inflammation. Significant shunting of blood to the venous system lowers arterial pressure, increases cardiac preload, and may produce high output cardiac failure. The renal artery is not commonly involved.`,`Berry (saccular) aneurysm is a congenital malformation that occurs at increased frequency in patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease. They are most common in CNS arteries and, with rupture, produce subarachnoid hemorrhage, which is often fatal.`,` Unilateral renal artery stenosis leading to renal “underperfusion” results in increased renin release, which in turn acts on plasma angiotensinogen I; the latter is converted to angiotensinogen II in the periphery, which causes secondary hypertension by causing vasoconstriction, aldosterone secretion, and increasing sodium retention by the kidney. Stenosis can be due to atherosclerosis or renal artery fibromuscular dysplasia. It is important to recognize that stenting of the obstruction can relieve hypertension and its long-term complications.`,`Fibrinoid necrosis is often seen with severe forms of vasculitis, such as polyarteritis nodosa. Such patients usually have signs of systemic inflammation.`],
