        optionFeedback: [`Acetaminophen has no effect on platelet function.`,` Aspirin is a potent inhibitor of platelet activation, due to its inhibitory effects on cyclo-oxygenase I (COX1), the enzyme that synthesizes the prostaglandin thromboxane A2. Platelet function is central to the formation of thrombi in conditions of high shear, such as those that exist in arteries, and the ability of aspirin to inhibit thrombosis lowers the risk of an obstruction due to the formation of a thrombus over a complex atherosclerotic plaque.`,`Ibuprofen, like aspirin, is an inhibitor of both COX1 and COX2, but unlike aspirin is not an irreversible inhibitor. The ability of aspirin to irreversibly inhibit COX1 makes it an especially potent platelet antagonist with favorable COX1 versus COX2 activities at low doses. This is important because COX2 produces lipoxins that are anti-inflammatory and may help protect against atherosclerotic vessel disease.`,`Corticosteroids do not inhibit platelets and have complex effects on the risk of atherosclerosis, in part because they tend to worsen glucose control in individuals with type II diabetes.`,`Insulin may improve glucose control but otherwise will not do much to lower risk in this context.`],
